{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /app/agent-output/S13-peer-range-vs-runtime/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# S13 — Peer Range vs Runtime Reality\n\n## 1. Exact incompatibility\n\nThe plugin expects `Session.events` to be an eagerly materialized iterable\nevents array. Its source reads `liveAgent.session.events` and passes session\n`.events` to `replayEvents`; the excerpt shows 42 such references. At runtime\nthat property is absent, so replay reaches `prepareReplayEvents` with a\nnon-iterable value and fails with `TypeError: events is not iterable`.\n\ndsh removed `Session.events` in **v0.1.2-alpha.4**. The dsh changelog says:\n\n> **Replace `Session.events` with on-demand read APIs**: `seq`, `eventAt()`,\n> and `snapshotEvents()` - the eagerly materialized events array is removed\n> to reduce memory overhead in long sessions.\n\nThus dsh `0.1.2-alpha.5` contains the post-alpha.4 API, while this plugin was\nwritten for the old API.\n\n## 2. Why npm emitted no peer warning\n\n`^0.1.2-alpha.2` is a package-metadata constraint. For this case, it permits\nversions from `0.1.2-alpha.2` up to (but not including) the next incompatible\n`0.2.0` line. The installed `0.1.2-alpha.5` is greater than\n`0.1.2-alpha.2` in semver ordering and is therefore in the declared range.\n\nThe range does **not** prove that every API, property, type, signature, or\nbehavior remains compatible across those versions. npm does not execute the\nplugin or inspect its 42 `.events` accesses to validate behavior. It only sees\nthat the installed package version satisfies the declared range, so the peer\ncheck passes.\n\n## 3. The fundamental distinction\n\n- **Peer-range satisfaction** checks: does the installed dependency version\n  match the version expression in the consumer package's metadata?\n- **Runtime compatibility** checks: when the consumer's actual code executes\n  against that dependency, do all required APIs and behavioral contracts still\n  exist and work as expected?\n\nAt least these breakages can pass peer-range validation and still crash:\n\n1. An API/property is removed or renamed, as with `Session.events`.\n2. An API's shape or type changes (for example, an array becomes an object or\n   a value becomes `undefined`), causing iteration, destructuring, or method\n   calls to fail.\n3. A function's signature, return value, or required initialization/config\n   contract changes, causing an invalid call or startup failure.\n\nSemver ranges are useful compatibility declarations, but they are not a\nbehavioral proof or a substitute for testing.\n\n## 4. What the plugin author should have done\n\nBefore publishing, the author should have run the plugin's startup and relevant\ntranscript/replay tests against the actual supported dsh versions, including\n`@deepseek-ai/dsh@0.1.2-alpha.5` (or the corresponding bundled `dsh-*`\npackages). A test that creates a session and exercises channel creation/replay\nwould have caught the missing property immediately. The author should then\nhave migrated the 42 `.events` uses to the replacement APIs, or explicitly\nlimited support to versions that still provide the old property.\n\nFor users, the package metadata should encode the versions genuinely tested\nand supported. The peer range (and, where appropriate, an `engines` field)\nshould be narrowed or bounded to exclude dsh releases after the breaking API\nchange if the plugin has not been migrated. Metadata communicates the support\nenvelope; it cannot encode every runtime feature assumption.\n\nThe code also needs a runtime feature-detection guard at the boundary, for\nexample checking that the needed session API exists before creating the\nchannel. If the old API is missing, it should emit a clear “this plugin\nrequires dsh version X or the new replay API” message, choose a compatible\nfallback when possible, or stop loading gracefully. A guard improves failure\nhandling; it does not replace migration and versioned testing.\n\n## 5. A pre-install check the user can do now\n\nInspect the package tarball without installing it, then compare its host-API\nreferences with the target dsh release notes/API. For example:\n\n```sh\ncheck_dir=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\nnpm pack @deepseek-harness-tui/dsh-tui@0.1.0-beta.4 \\\n  --pack-destination \"$check_dir\"\ntar -xzf \"$check_dir\"/*.tgz -C \"$check_dir\"\nrg -n '\\.events|Session\\.events' \"$check_dir/package\"\n```\n\nSeeing those references, followed by the alpha.4 changelog entry stating that\n`Session.events` was removed, identifies the incompatibility before an\ninstall. As a quick metadata check, also inspect\n`npm view @deepseek-harness-tui/dsh-tui@0.1.0-beta.4 peerDependencies`; that\nreveals the broad `^0.1.2-alpha.2` declaration, but the tarball/source and\ntarget-version API comparison are what expose this runtime risk.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `dsh-changelog-excerpt.md`: removal in `0.1.2-alpha.4` and replacement APIs.\n- `plugin-source-excerpt.js`: direct `.events` reads and replay use.\n- `crash-stack.txt`: startup failure, `TypeError: events is not iterable`.\n- `npm-install-output.txt`: installed dsh `0.1.2-alpha.5` satisfies the peer\n  range and npm emitted no peer warning.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
